**Goal** — Add a feature-scoped lock primitive (path, acquire, release, host- and age-aware staleness predicates) to `src/execution/feature-lock.ts`, reusing the checkout lock's exclusive-create and stale-reclaim guarantees and verifying ownership before release.

**Motivation** — Without a feature lock that mirrors the checkout lock's race-safe semantics, two worktrees of the same project could mutate the shared `~/.nax/<projectKey>/features/<feature>/` tree at once. The two predicates stay distinct because acquire and precheck ask different questions: a dead PID on a young lock is reclaimable by the first and not flagged by the second, so collapsing them would flip a verdict.

**Approach** — `featureLockPath`, `acquireFeatureLock`, `releaseFeatureLock`, `lockHost()`, and the two predicates `isLockReclaimable` and `isLockSuspect` (kept distinct because acquire asks "may I take this lock?" while precheck asks "is this lock suspiciously old?"), over a record carrying the holder, reusing the checkout lock's exclusive-create and stale-reclaim guarantees and verifying ownership before release.

**Scope** — In: `src/execution/feature-lock.ts` with `featureLockPath(outputDir, feature)`, `acquireFeatureLock`, `releaseFeatureLock`, `lockHost()`, `isLockReclaimable`, `isLockSuspect`, and `_featureLockDeps` seam. Out: lifecycle wiring (US-002), unlock/precheck wiring (US-003), identity predicate (US-004), status/run-id work (US-005), curator fix (US-006).

**Interface**
```typescript
// src/execution/feature-lock.ts
export interface FeatureLockRecord {
  pid: number;
  host: string;
  workdir: string;
  feature: string;
  runId: string;
  startedAt: string;
  timestamp: number;
}

export type FeatureLockResult =
  | { acquired: true }
  | {
      acquired: false;
      holder: { pid: number; host: string; workdir: string; feature: string; runId: string; startedAt: string; timestamp: number };
    };

export function featureLockPath(outputDir: string, feature: string): string;
// → <outputDir>/features/<feature>/nax.lock

export function lockHost(): string;
// → os.hostname()

export async function acquireFeatureLock(args: {
  outputDir: string;
  feature: string;
  workdir: string;
  runId: string;
}): Promise<FeatureLockResult>;
// Creates <outputDir>/features/<feature>/ when absent.
// Mirrors checkout-lock stale-reclaim (rename→verify→restore via tryExclusiveCreate).
// Writes record { pid, host, workdir, feature, runId, startedAt, timestamp }.
// Corrupt/unparseable on-disk record → warn-log, treat as stale, remove, retry.
// Uses O_CREAT|O_EXCL via the exported tryExclusiveCreate from lock.ts.
// Refusal carries the existing on-disk record's pid/host/workdir.

export async function releaseFeatureLock(args: {
  outputDir: string;
  feature: string;
  runId: string;
}): Promise<void>;
// Re-reads the lock file before unlinking; only removes when the on-disk record's runId equals the caller.
// Otherwise leaves the file in place (no-op).
// ENOENT → resolves silently.

export function isLockReclaimable(record: Partial<FeatureLockRecord> & { pid: number }, now: number): boolean;
// | Record | Verdict |
// |:--|:--|
// | host matches this machine (case-insensitive) or host absent | reclaimable only when PID is not alive |
// | host differs from this machine | reclaimable only at age ≥ 2 hours |
// Age resolved from timestamp when present, else startedAt.

export function isLockSuspect(record: Partial<FeatureLockRecord> & { pid: number }, now: number): boolean;
// | Record | Verdict |
// |:--|:--|
// | host matches this machine or host absent | not suspect while PID alive OR age < 2h |
// | host differs from this machine | not suspect while age < 2h; suspect at or beyond |

export const _featureLockDeps = {
  featureLockPath: featureLockPath as typeof featureLockPath,
};
```

**Failure handling** — Corrupt lock file → warn log, treat as stale, remove (matches checkout-lock behaviour at `src/execution/lock.ts`). Missing parent directory → create. On-disk `runId` mismatch at release → leave file in place, no-op.

**Goal** — Make the run lifecycle acquire the checkout lock then the feature lock in `setupRun`, release both in reverse at every release site, add `host` to the checkout record, and split `LockAcquisitionError` into two refusals that name their holder.

**Motivation** — Acquiring the feature lock alone is unsafe: it is project-scoped, so a single checkout could both own the feature lock and proceed to mutate the project tree without mutual exclusion against another nax process in the same directory. Acquiring only the checkout lock is what was wrong before. The two must be held together, in a fixed order, to avoid cycles; a partial acquire must always be unwound before the error escapes.

**Approach** — Acquire the checkout lock then the feature lock in `setupRun`, release both in reverse at both release sites, add `host` to the checkout record, and split `LockAcquisitionError` into two refusals that name their holder. Depends on US-001.

**Scope** — In: extend `_runSetupDeps` with `acquireLock` and `acquireFeatureLock`; replace single-lock acquire in `setupRun` with checkout→feature sequence; wrap so feature-lock refusal releases the checkout lock first; split `LockAcquisitionError` into two classes (or shape-discriminated refusal messages); have `acquireLock` carry the holding PID/host in its refusal and write `host` into its record; have both release sites (FIX-H16 inner catch in `run-setup-init.ts` and the always-release at the bottom of `run-cleanup.ts`) release both locks in reverse order. Out: tooling visibility (US-003), identity-by-remote (US-004), status-file workdir (US-005), curator fix (US-006).

